#{[17592186209468 :question/problem "The table shows the streaming bitrates, rounded to the nearest whole number, for a group of $57$ video sessions.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bitrate</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Sessions</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA new bitrate of $20$ is added to the dataset, making $58$ bitrates in total. Which statement best compares the mean and median of the new dataset to those of the original dataset?"] [17592186209485 :question/problem "In a school club membership report, the counts of members by club and gender are shown in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Male</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Female</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Robotics Club</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$44$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$46$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$90$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Debate Club</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$33$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$60$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Art Club</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$39$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$70$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Environmental Club</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$120$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$180$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$160$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$340$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich club has a male count that is closest to $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the total number of males in the report?"] [17592186209486 :question/problem "At a farmers’ market, the counts of shoppers by stall type and time of day are shown in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Morning</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Afternoon</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Produce</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$45$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$72$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Bakery</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$56$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$81$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Dairy</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$33$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$46$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$79$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Flowers</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$66$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$107$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$200$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$139$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$339$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich stall type has a Morning count that is closest to $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the total number of Morning shoppers?"] [17592186212776 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Trays of Cookies</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Families</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFamilies were asked how many trays of cookies they baked during a holiday weekend. The table above summarizes the responses. What is the mean number of trays of cookies baked per family?"] [17592186212776 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the mean of a dataset given in a frequency table, we compute the sum of the products of each value $x$ and its corresponding frequency $f_x$, and then divide that sum by the total number of data points.\n\nWe begin by extending the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cookies ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Families ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$3 \\cdot 9 = 27\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$4 \\cdot 6 = 24\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$11 \\cdot 10 = 110\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$15 \\cdot 1 = 15\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$16 \\cdot 4 = 64\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{27 + 24 + 110 + 15 + 64}{9 + 6 + 10 + 1 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{240}{30} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean number of trays of cookies baked per family is $8$."] [17592186212793 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$r(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSome values of the linear function $r$ are shown in the table above. Which of the following defines $r(x)$?"] [17592186212797 :question/worked-solution "To find the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram, the horizontal axis shows the values in the dataset, and the vertical axis tells us how many times each value appears.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Score</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Students</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$76$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$77$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\nN = 3 + 5 + 7 + 3 + 2 = 20\n$$\n\nSince this is an even number, the median is the average of the values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N}{2} = \\dfrac{20}{2} = 10\\textrm{th} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad 10 + 1 = 11\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nLooking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $8$ positions in total.\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $15$ positions in total.\n\nSo, the $10$th and $11$th positions both fall in the third row and correspond to the value $78$. Therefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian &= \\frac{78 + 78}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{156}{2} \\\\\n&= 78points\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186212798 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12-15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18-21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $20$ total values in the dataset. Since this is an even number, the median is the average of the values in positions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{2} = 10 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad 10 + 1 = 11\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $10$ positions in total.\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $13$ positions in total.\n\nSo, we have that\n\n- the $10$th value falls in the second row corresponding to the group $15-18$, while\n- the $11$th value falls in the third row corresponding to the group $18-21$.\n\nThe median could fall in either of these groups. Of the given options:\n\n- Option I, $17.5$, *falls* within the group $15-18$, so *could be* the median.\n- Option II, $20$, *falls* within the group $18-21$, so *could be* the median.\n- Option III, $22$, *doesn't fall* within either group $15-18$ or $18-21$, so *cannot be* the median.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [17592186212802 :question/worked-solution "We begin by constructing the frequency table from the given dot plot:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Exercises Completed</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Members</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo calculate the mean of a dataset given in a frequency table, we compute the sum of the products of each value $x$ and its corresponding frequency $f_x$, and then divide that sum by the total number of data points.\n\nNext, we extend the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Exercises Completed ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Members ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2 \\cdot 2 = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3 \\cdot 2 = 6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4 \\cdot 3 = 12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6 \\cdot 3 = 18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(A) &= \\frac{4 + 6 + 12 + 18}{2 + 2 + 3 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{40}{10} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3.5 < 4$, the mean for Center B is less than the mean for Center A.\n\nTherefore, a comparison of the means suggests members of Center A completed more exercises per session on average."] [17592186212804 :question/worked-solution "The median is the middle value in an ordered data set. It separates the lower half from the upper half.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nTo find the median for Class B, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Score Range</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Students</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50-60$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$70-80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80-90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nClass B has a total of $18$ students. Since $18$ is even, the median is the average of the $9$th and $10$th values in the ordered data set.\n\nBoth the $9$th and $10$th values fall in the group $70-80$.\n\nSo, the median score in Class B lies in the group ${70-80}$.\n\nSince every score in the group $70-80$ is greater than $67$, the median score in Class B is greater than the median score in Class A.\n\nBased on the median values, students in Class B performed better on the quiz."] [17592186212805 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Grade</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA teacher graded the results of a math test using a $4$-point scale. The distribution of the grades is shown in the table above. Find the missing values in the cumulative frequency column."] [17592186212805 :question/worked-solution "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies.\n\nTo complete the cumulative frequency column, we start with the first value. Then, we walk down the table, adding each new frequency to the cumulative frequency in the previous row.\n\nFor the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Grade</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative frequency *from the previous row*. So, we have the following:\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $2$nd row in our table is equal to\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}+{\\color{red}5}=7\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Grade</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $3$rd row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{red}7}=14\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Grade</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}7}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}7}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $4$th row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} + {\\color{red}14} = 20\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Grade</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}14}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212806 :question/problem "A group of students were asked how many snacks they purchased from the school vending machine last week. The frequency table below shows the distribution of responses. Fill in the missing values in the cumulative frequency table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Snacks Bought</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212806 :question/worked-solution "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nTherefore, to find the frequency for each row except the first one, we subtract the previous cumulative frequency from the current cumulative frequency.\n\n- The frequency for the $1$st row in our table is equal to $3$.\n- The frequency for the $2$nd row in our table is equal to $12-3=9$.\n- The frequency for the $3$rd row in our table is equal to $18-12=6$.\n- The frequency for the $4$th row in our table is equal to $25-18=7$.\n\nLet's add these values to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Snacks Bought</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{9}=12-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{6}=18-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">$\\qquad\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}=25-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212811 :question/problem "An IT support team recorded how many tickets individual technicians resolved during a shift. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Tickets Resolved</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat are the mode(s) of this data set?"] [17592186212811 :question/worked-solution "The mode is the value with the highest frequency in the data set.\n\nFrom the frequency table, we see that the highest frequency is $6$, corresponding to resolving $4$ and $7$ tickets.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Tickets Resolved</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the data set has two modes: $4$ and $7$."] [17592186212812 :question/problem "A music club recorded the number of songs each student memorized over a month. The incomplete data set is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Songs Memorized</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf the only mode of the data set is $2$, which of the following values could be the value of $x$?\n\n1. $7$\n2. $8$\n3. $6$"] [17592186212815 :question/problem "A fitness coach records how many flights of stairs each participant climbs in a building. The cumulative frequency table below summarizes the data. Fill in the missing values in the table and determine the median number of stairs climbed.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Flights Climbed</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{3}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{11}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212815 :question/worked-solution "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing value in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Flights Climbed</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{11}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values in the dataset is $11$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since our dataset has an odd number of data points, the median is the middle value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian position &= \\frac{11 + 1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{2} \\\\\n&= 6th\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $6$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $5$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $11$ positions in total.\n\nSo, the $6$th position falls into the fourth row that corresponds to the value $8$ (stairs climbed).\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Flights Climbed</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{11}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the median number of stairs climbed is\n\n$$\n\\text{Median} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{8}\n$$"] [17592186212816 :question/problem "A group of volunteers reported how many hours they spent helping out during a weekend event. The cumulative frequency table below summarizes the data. Fill in the missing values in the table and determine the median number of volunteer hours.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Volunteer Hours</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{2}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{7}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 2px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{20}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212816 :question/worked-solution "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing value in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Volunteer Hours</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{20}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values in the dataset is $20$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since our dataset has an even number of data points, the median is the average of the two middle values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{2} = 10\\textrm{th} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 10+1 = 11\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $10$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $20$ positions in total.\n\nSo, we have that\n\n- The $10$th value falls in the third row corresponding to the value $5$ (volunteer hours), and\n- The $11$th value falls in the fourth row corresponding to the value $9$ (volunteer hours).\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Volunteer Hours</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{7}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{20}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian &= \\frac{5 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{2} \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvolunteer hours."] [17592186212817 :question/problem "The incomplete frequency table below shows how many days guests stayed at a mountain lodge.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Days</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Guests</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,? \\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf the mean number of days stayed was $12$, how many guests stayed for $14$ days?"] [17592186212817 :question/worked-solution "To find the missing frequency, we can find an expression for the mean in terms of the missing frequency.\n\nWe first extend the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Days ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Guests ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$1 \\cdot 2 = 2\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$2 \\cdot 2 = 4\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$6 \\cdot 2 = 12\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f_{14}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$14 \\cdot f_{14}\\qquad\\:\\:$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we find an expression for the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{2 + 4 + 12 + 14f_{14}}{2 + 2 + 2 + f_{14}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18 + 14f_{14}}{6 + f_{14}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the mean of the dataset is $12$. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18 + 14f_{14}}{6 + f_{14}} = 12\n$$\n\nThen, we eliminate the denominator by multiplying both sides by $(6 + f_{14})$, before solving for $f_{14}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 + 14f_{14} &= 12(6 + f_{14}) \\\\\n18 + 14f_{14} &= 72 + 12f_{14} \\\\\n14f_{14} - 12f_{14} &= 72 - 18 \\\\\n2f_{14} &= 54 \\\\\nf_{14} &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a total of $27$ guests stayed for $14$ days."] [17592186212821 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Movies Watched</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Friends</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTwo groups of friends reported the number of movies they watched last month. The table above summarizes the responses for Group A. For Group B, the most frequently reported number of movies watched was $4$. A comparison of the modes suggests which group watched more movies last month?"] [17592186212821 :question/worked-solution "The mode is the value with the highest frequency in the data set.\n\nFrom the frequency table, we see that the highest frequency is $7$, corresponding to $2$ movies watched.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Movies Watched</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Friends</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the mode for Group A is $2$. And since $2 < 4$, the mode for Group B is greater than the mode for Group A.\n\nTherefore, based on the modes, Group B watched more movies last month."] [17592186212822 :question/problem "A farmer recorded the number of apples picked from each tree. The incomplete data set is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Apples Picked</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf $3$ is a mode of the data set, which of the following values could be the value of $x$?\n\n1. $7$\n2. $8$\n3. $6$"] [17592186212823 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Items Purchased</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td></td>\n<td data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Items Purchased</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$33$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$38$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTwo grocery stores, store $A$ and store $B$, recorded how many items customers purchased in a day. The cumulative frequency tables above summarize the data from each store, where the data from store $A$ is on the left, and store $B$'s data is on the right. Which store has the smaller median?"] [17592186212832 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cups of Water Drunk</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Campers</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTwo groups of campers reported the number of cups of water they drank during a hike. The table above summarizes the responses for Group A. For the campers in Group B, their average number of cups of water was $2$.\n\nA comparison of the means suggests which group drank more cups of water on average?"] [17592186212832 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the mean of a dataset given in a frequency table, we compute the sum of the products of each value $x$ and its corresponding frequency $f_x$, and then divide that sum by the total number of data points.\n\nWe begin by extending the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cups of Water ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Campers ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1 \\cdot 5 = 5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3 \\cdot 3 = 9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5 \\cdot 3 = 15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7 \\cdot 1 = 7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(A) &= \\frac{5 + 9 + 15 + 7}{3 + 3 + 3 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{12} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 < 3$, the mean for Group B is less than the mean for Group A.\n\nTherefore, a comparison of the means suggests Group A drank more cups on average."] [17592186212838 :question/problem "A corporation randomly sampled $500$ employees about next year’s health insurance. The results were categorized by work arrangement and are shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Plan to choose<br>Health Plan A</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Plan to choose<br>Health Plan B</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Remote</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$110$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">On-site</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$140$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$160$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe corporation has $34{,}000$ employees in total. Based on the table, what is the best estimate of the number of employees who plan to choose Health Plan A?"] [17592186212878 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Full-Time</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Part-Time</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Contract</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Managers</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$40$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Assistants</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$37$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$77$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows the employment types of different groups of employees. If an employee from the Managers group is selected at random, what is the probability that they are either full-time or contract?"] [17592186212878 :question/worked-solution "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of employees who are full-time or contract among those who are managers:\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Full-Time or Contract} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Managers}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Managers working full-time or contract}}{\\text{# Managers}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of $\\color{red}{40}$ managers are listed. Of these,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{21}} + {\\color{blue}{9}} = 30\n$$\n\nare full-time or contract employees.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Full-Time</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Part-Time</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Contract</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Managers</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}{21}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{red}{40}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Assistants</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$37$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$77$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the fraction of employees who are full-time or contract among those who are managers is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Full-Time or Contract} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Managers}) = \\dfrac{30}{40} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"] [17592186212880 :question/problem "A survey records whether people are vegetarians and whether they prefer organic food. The data is summarized in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Prefers Organic</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Prefers Conventional</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Vegetarians</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Non-Vegetarians</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nComplete the frequency table given that, if a person who prefers conventional food is selected at random, the probability that the person is a vegetarian is $\\dfrac{3}{8}$."] [17592186212880 :question/worked-solution "We are told that\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Vegetarian} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Conventional}) = \\dfrac38\n$$\n\nThis probability is the fraction of vegetarians among those who prefer conventional food:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Vegetarian} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Conventional}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Vegetarians who prefer conventional food}}{\\text{# People who prefer conventional food}}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of vegetarians who prefer conventional food. From the table, we can see that a total of\n$\\color{red}20 + x$\npeople prefer conventional food. Of these, $\\color{blue}x$ are vegetarians.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Prefers Organic</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Prefers Conventional</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Vegetarians</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$\\color{blue}{x}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Non-Vegetarians</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the fraction of vegetarians among those who prefer conventional food is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Vegetarian} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Conventional}) = \\dfrac{x}{20+x}\n$$\n\nSince this probability is equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac38\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{8} &= \\frac{x}{20 + x} \\\\\n3(20 + x) &= 8x \\\\\n60 + 3x &= 8x \\\\\n5x &= 60 \\\\\nx &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Prefers Organic</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Prefers Conventional</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Vegetarians</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{12}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Non-Vegetarians</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212920 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Concerts</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Festivals</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Sports</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Friends</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Families</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Couples</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows the attendance at different events by groups of people. If a couple is selected at random, what is the probability that they attended either concerts or sports?"] [17592186212920 :question/worked-solution "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of couples who attended either concerts or sports:\n\n$$\n\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! P(\\text{Concerts or Sports} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Couples}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Couples attending concerts or sports}}{\\text{# Couples}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of\n\n$$\n20 + 18 + 24 = {\\color{red}62}\n$$\n\ncouples were surveyed. Of these,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{20}} + {\\color{blue}{24}} = 44\n$$\n\nattended either concerts or sports.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Concerts</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Festivals</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Sports</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Friends</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Families</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Couples</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$\\color{blue}{20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$\\color{blue}{24}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the fraction of couples who attended either concerts or sports is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Concerts or Sports} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Couples}) = \\dfrac{44}{62} = \\dfrac{22}{31}\n$$"] [17592186212926 :question/problem "The table below shows the daily water usage (in liters) for five households.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Household</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Water (L)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA sixth household uses $12$ liters, and this value is added to the original dataset of five households to create a new dataset. Which dataset has a greater mean?"] [17592186212969 :question/problem "The table below shows the distribution of **running lap times (in minutes)** recorded at a track meet. Estimate the mean lap time.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Lap Time $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Runners $(f)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12-14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14-16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186212969 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the mean from grouped data, we first use the frequency table.\n\nSince the data is grouped, we record $x_{\\textrm{mid}}$, the middle value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{mid}}$ for each row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Lap Time $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Runners $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{mid}}$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{mid}}\\cdot f$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4\\cdot 9 = 36$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5\\cdot 11 = 55$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12-14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6\\cdot 13 = 78$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14-16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\cdot 15 = 45$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2\\cdot 17 = 34$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, our estimate of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{36 + 55 + 78 + 45 + 34}{4 + 5 + 6 + 3 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{248}{20} \\\\\n&= 12.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean lap time is approximately $12.4$ minutes."] [17592186212970 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30-33$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$33-36$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36-39$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows how many values from a dataset fall into each group. Which of the following values could be the median of the dataset?\n\n1. $25$\n2. $31$\n3. $34$"] [17592186212970 :question/worked-solution "The cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add a cumulative frequency column and fill in the values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30-33$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$33-36$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36-39$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$26$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $26$ total values in the dataset. Since this is an even number, the median is the average of the values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{26}{2} = 13\\textrm{th} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad 13 + 1 = 14\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $13$th and $14$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $13$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $20$ positions in total.\n\nSo, we have that\n\n- the $13$th value falls in the third row corresponding to the group $30-33$, while\n- the $14$th value falls in the fourth row corresponding to the group $33-36$.\n\nThe median could fall in either of these groups. Of the given options:\n\n- Option I, $25$, doesn't fall within either group $30-33$ or $33-36$, so cannot be the median.\n- Option II, $31$, falls within the group $30-33$, so could be the median.\n- Option III, $34$, falls within the group $33-36$, so could be the median.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [17592186213550 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$. There is a linear relationship between $x$ and $y$. Which of the following equations represents this relationship?"] [17592186213571 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$. There is a linear relationship between $x$ and $y$. Which of the following equations represents this relationship?"] [17592186213579 :question/problem "A teacher recorded the time that some students in a class spent studying for a final exam. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours Studied</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat are the mode(s) of this data set?"] [17592186213584 :question/problem "Consider two datasets with t