-cell-align=\"center\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$218$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$230$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$220$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nA sixth store recorded $240$ sales, and this value is added to the original dataset of five stores to create a new dataset. Complete the following statement so that it correctly compares the means of the two datasets.\n\nThe mean of the original dataset is\n{{field-1}}\nthe mean of the new dataset because the new value is\n{{field-2}}\nthe mean of the original dataset."] [17592186213781 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Drives Car</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Takes Public Transit</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">City Residents</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Suburban Residents</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows the commuting methods of different residents. If a suburban resident is selected at random, what is the probability that they take public transit?"] [17592186213790 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30 - 32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32 - 34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$34 - 36$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36 - 38$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$38 - 40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Estimate the mean of this data set."] [17592186213791 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8 - 10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10 - 12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12 - 14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14 - 16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16 - 18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. In which group does the median of the dataset lie?"] [17592186213792 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12 - 15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 - 18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18 - 21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21 - 24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24 - 27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values from a dataset that fall into each group. Which of the following values could be the median of the dataset?\n\n1. $16$\n2. $22$\n3. $25$"] [17592186213796 :question/problem "The table below shows the distribution of **sleep duration per night (in hours)** among adults in a wellness survey. Estimate the mean sleep duration.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sleep Duration $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Adults $(f)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4 - 5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5 - 6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6 - 7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7 - 8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8 - 9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186214236 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the same data set once more, but this time we'll use cumulative frequency to calculate the median.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe first need to construct the corresponding *cumulative frequency* table. So, let's start by adding another column to store our cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRecall that the cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing values in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values in the dataset is $7$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since our dataset has an odd number of data points, the median is the middle value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian position &= \\frac{7 + 1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{2} \\\\\n&= 4th\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $4$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see that the first row of the table covers the first $4$ positions in total.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the $4$th position falls into the first row that corresponds to the value $3$. Therefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\text{Median} = 3\n$$\n\nIn this example, the position we wanted (the $4$th position) coincided with a value in the cumulative frequency column. When this doesn't happen, we need to think a little more carefully about which value in the table to select as the median.\n\nLet's see an example."] [17592186214237 :tutorial/content "Recall that the *mode* of a data set is the value that occurs most often in a data set. In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the mode of a data set presented as a frequency table.\n\nConsider a data set with the corresponding frequency table shown below. What is the mode of this data set?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the table, we see that the highest frequency is $7$, corresponding to the value $8$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the mode of the data set is $8$.\n\nRecall that some data sets have more than one mode. Let's see an example of how this looks when analyzing frequency tables."] [17592186214238 :tutorial/content "When *grouped* data is displayed in a histogram, we can only *estimate* the median. This is because we don't know the exact values that make up our data set.\n\nTo estimate the median in such cases, we create a cumulative frequency table as before, and then find the *group* in which the median lies.\n\nLet’s see how this works with an example. In which group does the median of the following dataset lie?\n\n![](images/89/89e9758d3acf07c56186fcabb0fa99af3c0befa091ec2dc4bd9df15cad26758b.png)\n\nThe cumulative frequency table corresponding to this histogram is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12-15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18-21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\n1+2+3+6+5=17\n$$\n\nSince $17$ is an odd number, the median is in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{17+1}{2}=\\dfrac{18}{2}=9\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $9$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $6$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $9$th value falls in the fourth interval, which is\n$18-21$.\n\nSo, the true median lies somewhere between $18$ and $21$."] [17592186214243 :tutorial/content "A **cumulative frequency table** is an extension of a regular frequency table that shows how frequencies *accumulate* across an *ordered data set*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's fill in the missing values in the following cumulative frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe cumulative frequency column shows the *running total* of the frequencies.\n\nTo complete the cumulative frequency column, we start with the first value. Then, we walk down the table, adding each new frequency to the cumulative frequency in the previous row.\n\nFor the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative frequency *from the previous row*.\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $2$nd row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}+{\\color{red}3}=5\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $3$rd row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{red}5}=6\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}5}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $4$th row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{red}6} = 13\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}7}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis gives us our completed cumulative frequency table."] [17592186214246 :tutorial/content "We've seen how it's often convenient to organize data in the form of a frequency table. We've also seen that the mean of a data set gives a measure of its centrality. In this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean of a data set when presented as a frequency table.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's discuss how to compute the mean of the data set given by the frequency table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf we were to write the data set out in full, we'd get the following:\n\n$0,\\:\\: 0, \\:\\: 1, \\:\\: 1, \\:\\: 1, \\:\\: 1, \\:\\: 2, \\:\\: 2, \\:\\: 3, \\:\\: 3, \\:\\: 3, \\:\\: 3, \\:\\: 3, \\:\\: 3$\nNotice that we have $14$ data points in total. So, to calculate the mean, we must sum these values and then divide the result by $14$.\n\nWe can make the computation a little faster by taking advantage of the repetition in the data set. Since the value $0$ occurs twice, $1$ occurs four times, $2$ occurs twice, and $3$ occurs six times, we can calculate the mean as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 6}{14} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 4 + 4 + 18}{14} \\\\\n&= \\frac{26}{14} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.86\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the same as multiplying the value in each row by its respective frequency, summing the results for each row, and then dividing by the total number of data points."] [17592186214247 :tutorial/content "When a data set contains an even number of data points, the median is the mean of the two middle values.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works when the data are presented using a frequency table, consider the following cumulative frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe total number of values in the dataset is $10$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since $10$ is even, the median is the average of the two middle values in the following positions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{2} = 5\\textrm{th} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 5+1 = 6\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $5$th and $6$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $3$ positions in total.\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.\n\nSo, the $5$th and $6$th positions both fall in the third row and correspond to the value $8$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian &= \\frac{8 + 8}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{2} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen dealing with an even number of data points, we sometimes need to find the mean of values that lie in two separate groups! Let's see an example."] [17592186214248 :tutorial/content "Now that we've learned how to calculate the mean of a data set from a frequency table, let's consider how we'd calculate a missing frequency given the mean of a data set.\n\nConsider the frequency table below, where the frequency associated with the value $x=9$ is unknown.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f_x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we're given that the mean of the dataset is $10$. How can we use this to find the missing frequency?\n\nTo find the missing frequency, let's first extend the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency, as before.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$2 \\cdot 2 = {\\color{red}{4}}\\:\\:\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$6 \\cdot 2 = {\\color{red}{12}}\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}f_{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">${\\color{red}{9\\cdot f_9}}\\:\\:\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$13 \\cdot 9 = {\\color{red}{117}}\\:\\:$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that we've denoted the missing frequency as $f_9$.\n\nNow, we find an expression for the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{4 + 12 + 9f_{9} + 117}{2 + 2 + f_{9} + 9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{133 + 9f_{9}}{13 + f_{9}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the mean of the dataset is $10$. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{133 + 9f_{9}}{13 + f_{9}} = 10\n$$\n\nTo compute the missing frequency, we must solve this rational equation.\n\nFirst, we eliminate the denominator by multiplying both sides by $(13 + f_{9})$ before solving for $f_{9}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n133 + 9f_{9} &= 10(13 + f_{9}) \\\\\n133 + 9f_{9} &= 130 + 10f_{9} \\\\\n9f_{9} - 10f_{9} &= 130 - 133 \\\\\n- f_{9} &= -3 \\\\\nf_{9} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the missing frequency, we can complete the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186214253 :tutorial/content "What is the median of the dataset given by the histogram below?\n\n![](images/77/77171f8a629b903e890e3eeebd3bc0b5b0f73a029195f65b659769a380118a7c.png)\n\nTo find the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a *cumulative frequency* table.\n\nIn our case, the cumulative frequency table corresponding to this histogram is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values in our data set equals $15$. Since this is an odd number, the median is the value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15 + 1}{2} = \\frac{16}{2} = 8th\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $8$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- The first row of the table covers the first $6$ positions.\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $9$ positions in total.\n\nSo, the $8$th value falls in the second row and corresponds to the value $1.0$. Therefore, the median is\n\n$$\nMedian = 1.0\n$$"] [17592186214280 :tutorial/content "When calculating the mean, it's easy to make a mistake when the frequency table is large or contains big numbers. For that reason, it's useful to proceed in stages.\n\nLet's consider the same data set once more, but this time we'll proceed in stages.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe begin by extending the table to include a third column for the product of each value and its frequency. We'll denote each possible value in the table by $x$ and the associated frequency $f_x$, as shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we calculate the product $x\\cdot f_x$ in each row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0 \\cdot 2 = 0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1 \\cdot 4 = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2 \\cdot 2 = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3 \\cdot 6 = 18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now calculate the mean by dividing the sum of these products by the total number of data points, given by the total sum of the frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{0 + 4 + 4 + 18}{2 + 4 + 2 + 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{26}{14} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.86\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186214281 :tutorial/content "It's often convenient to organize data in the form of a frequency table. We've also seen that the median of a data set gives a measure of its centrality. In this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the median of a data set when presented as a frequency table.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's discuss how to compute the median of the data set given by the frequency table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf we were to write the data set out in full, we'd get the following:\n\n$$\n3, \\qquad 3, \\qquad 3, \\qquad 3, \\qquad 7, \\qquad 10, \\qquad 13\n$$\n\nNotice that the data is ordered, and there are $7$ data points in total. Since $7$ is an odd number, the median is given by the\n\n$$\n(7+1)/2 = 4\n$$\n\nth element in the ordered list.\n\nSo, we count the elements from the left until we get to the $4$th element.\n\n$$\n3, \\qquad 3, \\qquad 3, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf 3}}, \\qquad 7, \\qquad 10, \\qquad 13\n$$\n\nSince the $4$th element is $3$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Median} = {\\color{blue}\\mathbf3}\n$$\n\nThe method of listing all the values in the data set becomes impractical when the frequencies are large. A more efficient way of doing this is to use *cumulative frequency.* We'll discuss this method next."] [17592186214282 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate conditional probabilities from frequency tables. The key is to recognize that we will usually be comparing two counts:\n\n- the number of outcomes that satisfy both the *event* $(A)$ and the *condition* $(B)$, and\n- the number of outcomes that satisfy the condition alone.\n\nRecall that in symbols, the conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{\\#(A \\cap B)}{\\#(B)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\#$ denotes the function that returns the number of elements in the corresponding set.\n\nFor example, the table below shows the distribution of participants’ workout durations.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Workout Duration (minutes)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Participants</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0-19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20-39$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40-59$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf a participant who exercised at least $40$ minutes is selected at random, what is the probability that the participant exercised at least $60$ minutes?\n\nTo determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of participants who exercised at least $60$ minutes among those who exercised at least $40$ minutes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&P(\\text{Exercised}\\ge60\\mid\\text{Exercised}\\ge40) \\\\ &= \\frac{\\substack{\\text{\\# participants who exercised}\\\\\\text{at least 40 and at least 60}}}{\\substack{\\text{\\# participants who exercised}\\\\\\text{at least 40}}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\substack{\\text{\\# participants who exercised}\\\\\\text{at least 60}}}{\\substack{\\text{\\# participants who exercised}\\\\\\text{at least 40}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total number of participants who exercised at least $40$ minutes is the sum of the participants in the $40 - 59$, $60 - 79$, and $80 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12 + 9 + 4} = {\\color{blue}25}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Workout Duration (minutes)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Participants</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0-19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20-39$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40-59$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOn the other hand, the number of participants who exercised at least $60$ minutes is the sum of the participants in the $60 - 79$ and $80 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}9} + {\\color{red}4} = {\\color{red}13}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Workout Duration (minutes)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Participants</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0-19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20-39$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40-59$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the fraction of participants who exercised at least $60$ minutes out of those who exercised at least $40$ minutes is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Exercised} \\geq 60 \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Exercised} \\geq 40) = \\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}25}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** A common mistake is dividing by the grand total instead of the conditional group. So, we always identify the condition first, then narrow down to the event."] [17592186214284 :tutorial/content "So far, we have seen how to calculate conditional probabilities from a one-way frequency table. But many real data sets are organized by two features at once. In such cases, we summarize the data in a *joint frequency table*.\n\nThe formula remains the same:\n\n$$\nP(A \\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{\\#(A \\cap B)}{\\#(B)}\n$$\n\nBut now, the events $A$ and $B$ often refer to different categories from different features.\n\nFor example, the table below shows the number of people by group and library membership.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Has Library Card</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">No Library Card</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Students</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$42$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Adults</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$42$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$46$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$38$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$84$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf an adult is selected at random, what is the probability that the adult does not have a library card?\n\nTo determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of adults without a library card among all adults:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{No card} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Adult}) = \\dfrac{\\text{# Adults without a card}}{\\text{# Adults}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of $\\color{red}{42}$ adults responded. Of these, $\\color{blue}{24}$ do not have a card.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Has Library Card</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">No Library Card</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Students</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$42$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Adults</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}{24}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{red}{42}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$46$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$38$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$84$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the fraction of adults without a library card among all adults is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{No card} \\: \\mid \\: \\text{Adult}) = \\dfrac{24}{42} = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$"] [17592186214320 :tutorial/content "When a frequency table contains grouped data, we cannot get an exact value for the mean because we don't have access to the full data set. However, we can *estimate* the mean using a similar method to what we saw before.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's estimate the mean of the data set represented by the frequency table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-62$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$62-64$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64-66$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$66-68$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$68-70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince the data is grouped, we start by introducing $x_{\\textrm{mid}}$, the middle value of $x$ for each group.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{mid}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-62$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$62-64$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64-66$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$66-68$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$68-70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo calculate $x_{\\textrm{mid}}$ for each group, we simply calculate the mean of the values on the boundary of each group.\n\nFor example, the first group is $60-62$. The boundary values are $60$ and $62$, and so finding the mean of these values, we get\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{mid}} = \\dfrac{60+62}{2} = 61\n$$\n\nand we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{mid}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-62$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$61$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$62-64$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64-66$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$66-68$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$68-70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf we calculate $x_{\\textrm{mid}}$ for all groups, we get the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{mid}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-62$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$61$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$62-64$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$63$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64-66$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$65$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$66-68$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$67$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$68-70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$69$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we introduce a new column $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{mid}}$, and calculate this quantity for each row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{mid}}$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{mid}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60-62$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$61$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\cdot 61 = 183$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$62-64$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$63$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7\\cdot 63 = 441$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64-66$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2\\cdot 65 = 130$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$66-68$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$67$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6\\cdot 67 = 402$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$68-70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$69$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6\\cdot 69 = 414$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, our estimate of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{183 + 441 + 130 + 402 + 414}{3 + 7 + 2 + 6 + 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1570}{24} \\\\\n&= 65.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186214321 :tutorial/content "When data values are too numerous to list individually, we can group them into intervals and record how many fall into each. This arrangement is called a frequency table.\n\nTo analyze such data, we often extend the table with a cumulative frequency column, which helps locate measures like the median and quartiles. Let’s see how this works in the following example.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$72-76$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$76-80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80-84$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$84-88$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$88-92$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. In which group does the median of the dataset lie?\n\nFirst, notice that the cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing value in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$72-76$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$76-80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80-84$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$84-88$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$88-92$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}17}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values is ${\\color{blue}17}$. Since this is an odd number, the median is the value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}17} + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{18}{2} = {\\color{red}9\\textrm{th}}\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $9$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $8$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $9$th value falls in the **fourth** interval, which is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}84-88}\n$$"] [17592186215387 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215388 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215389 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215390 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215391 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215394 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215395 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186215396 :answer/value "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-al